    },
    {
      "id": "glZPuKcg4F2PmEVwefsAT",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1331.4892159127426,
      "y": 105443.20692597446,
      "width": 1188,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bw0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1225330365,
      "version": 810,
      "versionNonce": 39811442,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781398945275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Due to state values are in form of key:value pair, LangChain doesn't give any option to store and track\nsuch key value pairs. LangChain has concept of memory but this memory is conversational memory, means we\ncan store the chating that we have done with LLM, and we can send this in chain, so that our LLM know\nwhat was the previous conversation(context), but we can't store key value pair in LangChain. Meaning\nLangChain are stateless for these type of states due to it is not storing any state, If we want to implement\nthis kind of state then we need to do it manually.\n\nOne way we can treat these type of states as conversation text or we can implement and manage,track this\nmanually.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Due to state values are in form of key:value pair, LangChain doesn't give any option to store and track\nsuch key value pairs. LangChain has concept of memory but this memory is conversational memory, means we\ncan store the chating that we have done with LLM, and we can send this in chain, so that our LLM know\nwhat was the previous conversation(context), but we can't store key value pair in LangChain. Meaning\nLangChain are stateless for these type of states due to it is not storing any state, If we want to implement\nthis kind of state then we need to do it manually.\n\nOne way we can treat these type of states as conversation text or we can implement and manage,track this\nmanually.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1hB4xscADQna1dyPol96F",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1324.4071846627426,
      "y": 105712.22645722446,
      "width": 1232,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bw1",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1497964339,
      "version": 803,
      "versionNonce": 1887735470,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1781398945275,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "LangGraph execution around states is stateful,LangGraph is stateful, at the time of creation of graph \nin LangGraph we create a state object, which we can create using pydantic or typedDict, this state is\nbasically a Dictionary. the speciality of this dictionary is it can be accessed by every node of Graph. \nand not only node can read this dictionayr, they can update that also as dictionary is mutable. In LangGraph \ninformation passing is fantastic between nodes. it doesn't matter howmuch complex our state is we can represent \nit as dictionary.\nEx -> createJd node will create jd and update that in state as well likewise other node with do.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "LangGraph execution around states is stateful,LangGraph is stateful, at the time of creation of graph \nin LangGraph we create a state object, which we can create using pydantic or typedDict, this state is\nbasically a Dictionary. the speciality of this dictionary is it can be accessed by every node of Graph. \nand not only node can read this dictionayr, they can update that also as dictionary is mutable. In LangGraph \ninformation passing is fantastic between nodes. it doesn't matter howmuch complex our state is we can represent \nit as dictionary.\nEx -> createJd node will create jd and update that in state as well likewise other node with do.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
